# rocket agent guidelines

## Python coding guidelines

* Prioritize code correctness and clarity. Speed and efficiency are secondary priorities unless
  otherwise specified.
* Comments explain "why", not "what": do not write comments that summarize the code. Docstrings
  state contracts, concisely.
* Prefer implementing functionality in existing files unless it is a new logical component.
  Avoid creating many small files.
* Never silently discard errors. No bare `except:` and no `except Exception: pass`; raise a
  `RocketError` subclass with an actionable hint instead. The only broad `except Exception` is
  the per-item failure isolation in `cli.py`/`wizard.py`, which must still report the problem
  to the user.
* Guard upstream data explicitly: validate structure and raise a `RocketError` with a hint
  instead of relying on assertions or letting `KeyError`/`IndexError`/`TypeError` escape.
  Assertions belong in tests.
* CLI conventions: failures print `error: <message> (hint: ...)` to stderr; exit codes are
  0 success, 1 failures, 2 invalid config.
* Avoid creative additions unless explicitly requested: no new dependencies, commands, flags
  or config fields without asking first.
* Use full words for variable names (no abbreviations like `q` for `queue`).

## Project invariants (traps to avoid)

* Exact versions only: the download pipeline never resolves "latest", never accepts version
  ranges and never selects a version automatically. Only `rocket init` picks versions (and
  pins what it resolved), and `--upgrade` re-resolves locked items.
* Never invent download URLs and never treat an arbitrary web page as a JAR. External entries
  may only follow fixed-tag GitHub Release pages; failing with a hint is the correct behavior.
* Strict config validation is a feature: unknown fields, unknown providers and unknown
  resolvers are errors. Do not relax validation to be "friendly".
* Tests are fully offline (`httpx.MockTransport`/`FakeAPI`, temporary directories, fictional
  data). Never hit the network in tests and never weaken assertions just to make the suite pass.
* `uv.lock` must stay in sync; CI runs `uv sync --locked`.
* Downloads are validated (size, upstream hash when available, ZIP/JAR structure) before the
  atomic replace. Do not soften or skip validation.

## Workflow

* Run the four gates locally before every push: `uv run ruff format --check .`,
  `uv run ruff check .`, `uv run ty check`, `uv run pytest -q`.
* `main` is protected (ruleset `protect-main`): no direct pushes, required status checks
  `lint` + `test`, linear history, squash-only merges. Every change goes through a pull
  request — even one-line fixes.
* Git conventions in this colocated jj workspace: commits and bookmarks via `jj`, tags via `git`.
* HARD RULE: never merge a pull request on your own. Open the PR, report it, and wait for the
  user's explicit approval before running `gh pr merge` (the user may also merge it themselves).
* After user-visible changes merge, a release is due — follow `.agents/skills/release/SKILL.md`.

## PR hygiene

* Use a clear, correctly capitalized, imperative PR title (for example, `Add release skill`).
* Avoid conventional commit prefixes (`fix:`, `feat:`, ...) and trailing punctuation in titles.
* End the PR body with a `Release Notes:` section containing exactly one bullet:
  * `- Added ...`, `- Fixed ...` or `- Improved ...` for user-facing changes, or
  * `- N/A` for tooling-only or docs-only changes.
* Format it with a blank line after the heading:

```
Release Notes:

- N/A
```

## Rules hygiene

* Every agent session reads this file; keep it high-signal.
* A rule must be non-obvious, repeatedly encountered and specific enough to act on. No
  architectural descriptions (module layout, data flow) — they go stale. This file holds
  traps to avoid, not maps to follow.
* Do not bundle rules edits into feature work: note proposed additions in a "Suggested rules
  additions" section of the PR body. Rules changes land in dedicated commits after the user
  approves them.
